    {
      "match_id": 3845506,
      "reason": "corrupted_three_sixty_json",
      "file": "data/raw/statsbomb/data/three-sixty/3845506.json",
      "evidence": "json.JSONDecodeError: Expecting ',' delimiter: line 92794 column 3 (char 2637824). Root cause confirmed via byte-level scan: a single, isolated 16,384-byte (exactly one storage block) run of NUL (0x00) bytes overwrites real JSON content inside one freeze_frame record, splitting the token \"false\" into \"fa\"+NULs+\"lse\". Scanned all 4,661 raw JSON files in the project (4,235 events.json + 426 three-sixty.json); this is the only file containing any NUL byte. The sibling events.json for this match (2,700,919 bytes) parses successfully (3,095 records) -- only the three-sixty file is affected. No safe recovery is possible without inventing the lost content; a string-literal-aware top-level-record extraction recovers 2,761/2,762 other records independently, but the record overlapping the corruption is permanently lost.",
      "date_investigated": "2026-10-13",
      "report": "docs/data_quality_investigation_2026-10-13.md"
    },
    {
      "match_id": 3923880,
      "reason": "insufficient_three_sixty_coverage_for_team_reference_resolution",
      "file": "data/raw/statsbomb/data/three-sixty/3923880.json",
      "evidence": "File is valid, well-formed JSON (1,962 bytes, exactly 1 record) -- NOT corrupted. The single record correctly joins to a real event (event_uuid 7cc66fb5-4f8f-448a-8e70-e863dd24217f, a Pass by Congo DR) in the match's otherwise-normal 3,374-event events.json (period 1/2/5, i.e. this match went to penalties). However, this is 1/3,374 events (0.03%) of 360 coverage, versus a typical match's ~85%, and the ONE covered event belongs to only one of the two teams (team_id 4881). src/team_normalization.py::resolve_reference_team_id requires at least one 360-matched event from EACH team to pick a reference team; with only one team represented, it raises ValueError('Expected exactly two team ids in events, found [4881].'). Reproduced directly via build_match_dataset(match_id=3923880, maximum_time_difference=10.0) (unconditional failure -- this function always calls resolve_reference_team_id) and via src/animate_statsbomb_360_match.py's main() with --apply-team-normalization (same underlying function, same error). This is a genuine, reproducible processing failure caused by sparse source data, not a judgment call based on file size alone.",
      "date_investigated": "2026-10-13",
      "report": "docs/supervisor_data_quality_summary_2026-10-13.md"
    }
  ],
